## ARM optimized-routines `pow` — MIT

`crates/sundials_core/src/sundials_math.rs` contains a pure-Rust port of the
double-precision `pow` from ARM optimized-routines, taken via musl's
`src/math/pow.c`, `pow_data.c` and `exp_data.c`:

    Copyright (c) 2018, Arm Limited.
    SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT

It replaces the platform `f64::powf` because the reference outputs this port is
verified against were generated against glibc >= 2.28, which uses that same
algorithm. Platform libm implementations disagree with it by one ulp on rare
arguments, and inside the step-size heuristics a one-ulp difference forks the
whole trajectory: substituting this implementation turned three examples from
failing to byte-identical. See `VERIFICATION.md`.

The attribution and algorithm provenance are also recorded in a block comment
at the implementation site.

**Scope of this substitution — important.** The ported routine is ordinary
host-independent Rust; despite the "ARM optimized-routines" provenance it is
not an Apple-Silicon-specific or arm64-specific routine. On this project's
target it is not even a substitution for a foreign implementation: that same
algorithm is what glibc >= 2.28 ships as `sysdeps/ieee754/dbl-64/e_pow.c`,
and on x86-64 glibc dispatches to `__ieee754_pow_fma`, the same source built
with `-mfma -mavx2 -ffp-contract=fast`. The Rust routine is measured
bit-exact against that native `pow` — 0 mismatches over 25.9 million inputs
(`tools/pow_differential.sh`, `POW_FMA_EXACTNESS.md` §0).

It removes the host libm from the `pow` path **and only from the `pow`
path**. Every other host-dependent function the library and the examples use
— `sin`, `cos`, `asin`, `acos`, `atan`, `sinh`, `cosh`, `acosh`, `exp`, `ln`
— is still `f64`'s method, which Rust `std` documents as having unspecified
precision and forwards to whatever libm the host provides. On Linux that is
glibc; on macOS/arm64 it is Apple's libm; on Windows it is the Microsoft CRT,
and the three disagree in the last ulp. (`sqrt`, `mul_add`, `ceil`, `round`,
`abs` and `copysign` are IEEE-754 specified and identical everywhere.) That
residual host dependence, not this MIT-licensed routine, is why the
byte-identity evidence in `VERIFICATION.md` is scoped to Linux on x86-64
with glibc — the platform whose libm generated the upstream reference
outputs. See `README.md` § "Platform scope".

## glibc `rand()` — behaviourally reproduced

`crates/arkode_rs/examples/ark_analytic_lsrk_domeigest.rs`,
`ark_brusselator_lsrk_domeigest.rs` and
`ark_brusselator_lsrk_externaldomeigest.rs` each contain a ~40-line Rust
implementation of the classic BSD/glibc `rand()` TYPE_3 additive-feedback
generator (DEG 31, SEP 3, Lehmer/Schrage seeding, 310 discarded outputs,
result = state word >> 1). No glibc source is reproduced — the code is Rust
written against that algorithm description, and the block comment at each site
records it.

It exists for the same reason as the `pow` port: those examples feed
pseudo-random vectors to a dominant-eigenvalue estimator, so the generator's
sequence is output-observable, and the reference `.out` files were produced
against glibc's. Like the `pow` port, it is host-independent Rust. It is
mentioned here so that "only `pow` was taken off the host C library" is read
as the statement it is — a statement about *libm*, not about the whole C
runtime.


## ARM optimized-routines `exp` and `log` — MIT

`crates/sundials_core/src/sundials_libm.rs` contains pure-Rust ports of the
double-precision `exp` and `log` from ARM optimized-routines, taken via
musl's `src/math/exp.c`, `exp_data.c`, `log.c` and `log_data.c`:

    Copyright (c) 2018, Arm Limited.
    SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT

glibc >= 2.28 (`exp`) and >= 2.29 (`log`) ship the same algorithm as
`sysdeps/ieee754/dbl-64/e_exp.c` and `e_log.c`, and on x86-64 ifunc-dispatch
to the `-mfma -mavx2 -ffp-contract=fast` build of it. Both are measured
bit-identical to the host glibc by `tools/libm_differential.sh`
(1,000,000 samples per corpus; see `LIBM.md`).

## Original work in this repository — BSD-3-Clause, with the project

The remainder of `crates/sundials_core/src/sundials_libm.rs` is original to
this repository and is not derived from any other library:

  * the double-double arithmetic core (classical Dekker / Knuth / Bailey
    algorithms, which are published mathematics, not copied source);
  * the exact Payne-Hanek argument reduction over the bits of 2/pi;
  * `expm1`, `log1p`, `sin`, `cos`, `atan`, `asin`, `acos`, `sinh`, `cosh`
    and `acosh` built on those two;
  * every constant table they use, generated by
    `tools/gen_libm_constants.py` from Taylor series evaluated in Python's
    standard-library `decimal` module.

In particular, none of it is derived from glibc's IBM Accurate Mathematical
Library implementations of `sin`, `cos`, `atan`, `asin` and `acos`, which
are LGPL-2.1 and were deliberately not consulted. That is why those five
functions are correctly rounded here rather than bit-identical to glibc;
see `LIBM.md` section 2.
